      "id": "ETZ-01",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "One pole cannot accommodate near-one zeros of two distinct real characters",
      "anchor": "inputs-exceptional-four",
      "statement": "Uniformly in positive Q, two distinct nonprincipal characters of order two modulo Q cannot both have a real zero in [1−1/(20H log(2Q)),1), for H=3|C|+2C′+1. The two real values β₁ and β₂ may differ.",
      "hypotheses": "C is a uniform constant in the local zero-sum bound and C′≥0 in the right-half-plane logarithmic derivative estimate. Nonprincipality of each character and of their product is essential to the single-pole count.",
      "definitions": [
        "Dψ(σ)=Re(−L′(σ,ψ)/L(σ,ψ)). Its coefficient at n coprime to Q in the four-character sum is Λ(n)(1+χ₁(n))(1+χ₂(n)); all four terms vanish for a nonunit.",
        "ℓ=log(2Q)>1/2, e=1/(20Hℓ)≤1/10, σ=1+2e. The local expansion at t=0 sums zeros in |ρ−11/10|≤1/5 and works for 1<σ≤6/5."
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-exceptional-positive",
        "inputs-exceptional-budget"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "DZF-02"
      ],
      "correspondence": "All zeros of a nonprincipal character have Re ρ<1, so the real parts of their reciprocal distances from σ are positive. Retain each named zero with multiplicity at least one: each gives ≥1/(3e), while the principal allowance is 1/(2e). The product character gives at most Cℓ. Positivity contradicts the total upper bound 3Cℓ+C′−1/(6e)≤(H−1)ℓ−(10/3)Hℓ<0.",
