      "id": "ETZ-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Uniform uniqueness in one level and agreement on common units",
      "anchor": "inputs-exceptional-common-modulus",
      "statement": "Some absolute c>0 gives at most one character–zero pair in Re z≥1−c/log(Q(|Im z|+2)) for each integer Q≥1, with the principal pole excluded. The pair, if present, consists of a real nonprincipal quadratic character and a real simple zero. For any D≥3 and any two positive levels M₁,M₂≤D, two real nonprincipal quadratic characters with zeros 1−c/(3log D)<βᵢ<1 agree on all units of M₁M₂.",
      "hypotheses": "The assembled width is the minimum of the widths for the Riemann zeta function, nonreal characters, real characters at large and small imaginary part, distinct real characters, and 1/2. The cross-level conclusion concerns values on integers coprime to both moduli.",
      "definitions": [
        "For the small-height estimate put K₀=2C₀+max(C,0), W=10(K₀+1), T=W log(2Q), σ=1+1/T. The small-height and real-part clauses have constant 1/(10W); each zero in that range contributes at least 4T/5 to the local zero sum, whose total is bounded by (11K₀+10)log(2Q)<8T/5."
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "dependencies": [
        "ETZ-01",
        "DZF-01",
        "DZF-02",
        "DZF-03"
      ],
      "correspondence": "Two distinct zeros or a zero of multiplicity at least two would violate that strict inequality. Conjugation of a real Dirichlet character's L-function then excludes a nonreal zero. The global statement uses separate proofs for the other character and imaginary-part cases. The change-of-level formula preserves each zero; the common product level has log(2M₁M₂)≤3log D. Uniqueness at that level yields agreement on common units.",
