      "id": "ETZ-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "How the fixed-height moment uses the single surviving twist",
      "anchor": "inputs-exceptional-selection",
      "statement": "For each ε,A,B>0 there is Y₀ such that every Y≥Y₀ and every admitted progression (U,m,a) admits a natural s★ for which all admissible s≠s★ satisfy |∑_{p∈P}(p/s)|≤Y^(−B)|P|. For the maximal mean-square estimate take A=800K, B=K+1 for 0<ε≤1, K≥1. Choose s★ before y∈[Y,Y^K] and the common coefficient sequence κ.",
      "hypotheses": "An admissible s is odd, squarefree, 1<s≤Y^A, coprime to m. Admitted progressions require m>0, 8|m, (a,m)=1, (log Y)² log m/4≤log U, and Y^(ε/6)≤log U≤Y^(ε/5)(log Y)². This estimate also requires that each p|m satisfy p≤Y^(ε/5) or p>(Y^K)^400.",
      "definitions": [
        "D=mY^A and c₀=c/3. E(ms) is the optional pair supplied by the relative progression theorem. If E(ms)=some(χ,β) then χ²=1, χ≠1, L(β,χ)=0, and 1−1/(8log(ms))<β<1.",
        "The one potentially bad s must have such a pair with 1−β<c₀/log D and agreement χ(u)=(u/s) on units u≡1 mod m. If no s meets both tests the source uses s★=0.",
        "Away from this one s, misalignment forces exact cancellation of the exceptional main term; in the aligned case r=4log U exp(−c₀ log U/log D) bounds li(U^β)/li(U). The uniform parameter estimate gives 4r≤Y^(−B)/8."
      ],
      "equations": [],
